<h2>The Gulf's outsized role in Indian cardamom trade</h2>
<p>No other Indian spice export concentrates this heavily in one region. Calendar 2024 data shows the UAE (USD 80.5 million) and Saudi Arabia (USD 43.1 million) together accounting for roughly 61% of India's total USD 203.6 million cardamom exports — and adding Kuwait, Iraq and Oman pushes Gulf markets above 70% of total export value. If you're building a spice-sourcing strategy around Gulf demand specifically, cardamom is arguably the single clearest anchor product.</p>

<h2>Why cardamom, and why the Gulf?</h2>
<p>Cardamom holds particular cultural and culinary weight in Gulf cuisine — most visibly in qahwa, traditional Arabic coffee, where cardamom is essential rather than optional, alongside its use across a range of traditional dishes and desserts. That sustained, deeply rooted demand supports pricing that few other spice markets match: at roughly USD 22.5 per kg average export value, cardamom is by a wide margin the highest unit-value spice India exports, against a total 2024 volume of just 9,033 MT — a genuinely high-value, low-volume trade.</p>

<h2>How cardamom is graded</h2>
<p>Two factors drive grade and price: capsule size (commonly specified as 7mm, 7.5mm or 8mm+) and colour, with bright green capsules commanding a premium over duller or yellowed ones. Bold, well-filled capsules with strong aroma represent the top tier. Specify your target size and colour grade, since these vary considerably by buyer and end use — retail packaging often wants the brightest, largest capsules, while bulk culinary use may prioritise consistency over premium appearance.</p>

<h2>Where it comes from</h2>
<p>Kerala's Idukki district, in the Western Ghats, is India's principal cardamom-growing region, along with neighbouring hill areas that share the altitude and rainfall conditions the crop requires. This is a genuinely specialised growing region — cardamom doesn't have the wide geographic spread that a staple like rice or wheat does.</p>

<h2>Documentation for Gulf shipments</h2>
<p>Standard phytosanitary certificate and certificate of origin apply. Halal documentation is sometimes requested by Gulf buyers even for products, like cardamom, with no inherent halal-relevant processing step — if your importer specifies a required certifying body, tell us and we'll arrange documentation to match. See our <a href="/markets/middle-east-gcc/">Middle East &amp; GCC region guide</a> for the wider picture on ports, transit and payment norms.</p>

<h2>Storage and shelf life</h2>
<p>Cardamom is more sensitive to storage conditions than most spices — its essential oils, which carry the aroma buyers pay for, degrade with heat and light exposure over time. Store in cool, dark, dry conditions and expect noticeably better retained aroma from stock used within 12-18 months of packing versus older stock, even when moisture and other specifications remain technically within range. If your business holds inventory for extended periods, factor this into your ordering cadence — buying smaller, more frequent shipments often beats one large annual order for a product this aroma-sensitive.</p>
